HEADER
this comprises of name or brand and a series of navigation links.

Inside <header>, we put a <nav> (navigation) landmark. Inside <nav>, there are two distinct sides:

-Your Brand / Logo Link (Left Side): An <a> tag with href="#" (which scrolls to the top of the page) containing your name or brand mark.

-Your Navigation Links (Right Side): An unordered list (<ul>) containing list items (<li>), each holding an anchor link (<a>) that points to an id on your page—such as #projects, #about, and #contact.
          The two core list types in HTML are:

                  -Unordered List (<ul>): Used when the order of items does not matter (such as navigation links—Projects, About, Contact aren't a numbered countdown or step sequence). By default, browsers show bullet points (•) and add some left indentation.

                   -Ordered List (<ol>): Used when the sequence or order matters (such as step-by-step instructions or ranked steps). By default, browsers number the items (1., 2., 3.).

Inside either <ul> or <ol>, each individual item lives inside a list item (<li>) tag. A <ul> or <ol> should directly contain <li> elements, while the actual text or link (<a>) goes inside each <li>.

