LAYOUT OF THE HEADER
Right now, <nav> contains two direct children:

Your brand link (Evans Maingi)

Your unordered list (<ul>) containing projects, about, and contact

When you set display: flex; on <nav>, those two children become flex items sitting side by side on a single horizontal row instead of stacking vertically. Two alignment properties do the heavy lifting:

justify-content: space-between; — Pushes the first item (Evans Maingi) to the far left and the second item (<ul>) to the far right.

align-items: center; — Centers both items vertically along the same horizontal line so nothing looks lopsided.

Then, setting display: flex; on nav ul turns the three <li> elements inside it into a horizontal row too, while gap: 2rem; gives clean breathing room between each link without messy margin hacks.



CSS SELECTORS
A CSS selector is the pattern at the start of a rule that tells the browser which HTML elements on the page should receive those styles.

-Universal selector (*): Matches every element on the page. Best kept minimal—like your margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; reset—so you don't accidentally force styles onto things that shouldn't have them.

-Type / element selector (body, ul, a): Targets every HTML tag of that name. Great for setting broad base rules (like body background and font), though a bare a rule will affect every link on the page unless narrowed down.

-Class selector (.nav-link, .btn): Targets elements that have a matching class="..." attribute in HTML (prefixed with a dot .). Reusable across multiple elements and ideal for components like buttons, cards, or special links.

-ID selector (#hero, #projects): Targets a single unique element with a matching id="..." attribute in HTML (prefixed with a hash #). Because an id should only appear once per page, it has high specificity—usually best reserved for major page anchors/sections rather than repeating UI styles.

-Pseudo-class selector (:root, :hover, :focus): Targets an element in a special state or context rather than a plain tag name—such as :root for the top-level document root or :hover when the pointer rests over a link.

-Descendant combinator (header nav): A space between selectors matches nav anywhere inside header (whether directly inside it or nested deeper).

-Child combinator (header > nav): A > matches nav only if it is a direct child immediately inside header, not nested inside a wrapper element in between.

-Adjacent / general sibling (h1 + p or h1 ~ p): Matches elements that sit after another element sharing the same parent (+ for immediately next, ~ for any following sibling).







